### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should almost certainly take the Standard Deduction.** 
For the 2023 tax year, the standard deduction for a single filer is **$13,850**. 
To itemize, your eligible personal deductions would need to exceed that amount. Your listed personal deductions are:
*   **Charitable Donation:** $650
*   **State/Local Taxes (SALT):** Capped at $10,000 (this includes your MN state income tax).
*   **Unreimbursed W-2 Work Supplies:** Under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (TCJA), W-2 employees can **no longer deduct** unreimbursed employee expenses on their federal returns. *(Note: If these supplies were for your 1099 freelance work, they are fully deductible on Schedule C, not as an itemized deduction).*

Since $650 + your state taxes will likely be less than $13,850, the standard deduction is the better choice.

### 2. Freelance Income & Deductions (Schedule C)
Your $19,700 in 1099-NEC income means you are considered self-employed for that portion of your work. You can deduct business expenses against this income to lower your taxable business profit.
*   **Work Supplies ($1,120):** If these were used for your freelance editing/proofreading business, you can deduct the full amount here.
*   **Home Office Deduction:** Because you use the 180 sq ft room *exclusively* and *regularly* for your freelance business, you qualify for this deduction. You have two choices:
    *   **Simplified Method:** 180 sq ft × $5 = **$900 deduction**. (Very easy, requires no record-keeping of actual expenses).
    *   **Regular Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (180 sq ft / 920 sq ft = **~19.56%**). You can deduct 19.56% of your rent ($1,950 × 12 = $23,400 × 19.56% = **~$4,577**), plus 19.56% of your renter's insurance, utilities, and internet. This method yields a much higher deduction but requires you to track all expenses and use Form 8829.

### 3. "Above-the-Line" Deductions (Adjustments to Income)
You can take these deductions even if you take the standard deduction:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,900, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500** per year. *Note: This deduction phases out based on your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI). For 2023, the phase-out for single filers begins at $75,000 and completely phases out at $90,000. With your W-2, 1099, and investment income, your MAGI will likely fall right in the middle of this phase-out range, meaning your $2,500 deduction will be partially reduced.*
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You will owe self-employment tax (Medicare and Social Security) on your net freelance profit. You are allowed to deduct 50% of this tax amount as an adjustment to income.

### 4. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, your return will consist of the following main forms:
*   **Form 1040:** The main tax return document.
*   **Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income):** Used to report your business income, your student loan interest deduction, and your self-employment tax deduction.
*   **Schedule 2 (Additional Taxes):** Used to report your self-employment tax.
